        "131": {
            "connections": [
                []
            ],
            "do_onload": false,
            "gui_params": {
                "clickchange": false,
                "code": null,
                "connection_info": [],
                "debug": false,
                "default": "",
                "display_type": "text",
                "export_offset_x": 0,
                "export_offset_y": 0,
                "height": 156.0,
                "is_export": false,
                "layername": "1.1 App layout",
                "max_width": 600,
                "min_height": 12,
                "min_width": 12,
                "no_export": false,
                "obj_args": null,
                "obj_name": "text_065",
                "obj_state": 3,
                "obj_type": "text",
                "panel_enable": false,
                "panel_x": 66.0,
                "panel_y": 146.0,
                "panel_z": 0,
                "patch_x": 66.0,
                "patch_y": 146.0,
                "patch_z": 0,
                "position_x": 66.0,
                "position_y": 146.0,
                "position_z": 0,
                "properties": {},
                "save_in_preset": false,
                "scope": "__patch__",
                "style": {},
                "update_required": true,
                "value": "\n<b>The canvas</b>\n\n* The area this text is displayed in is the canvas. This is where you edit and operate patches. \n\n* The canvas is a stack of <b>pages</b> (only one page is visible at a time) with each page being made up of <b>tiles</b> that will always exactly fill the window. \n\n* Each tile can display a <b>patch</b>. There's only one kind of patch in mfp, and it corresponds to a single <tt>.mfp</tt> file.\n\n* Each patch can have <b>layers</b>. Only one layer can be displayed at a time in a patch.",
                "width": 583.0
            },
            "initargs": null,
            "midi_filters": null,
            "midi_mode": null,
            "name": "text_065",
            "osc_methods": [],
            "presets": {},
            "properties": {},
            "type": "text",
            "value": "\n<b>The canvas</b>\n\n* The area this text is displayed in is the canvas. This is where you edit and operate patches. \n\n* The canvas is a stack of <b>pages</b> (only one page is visible at a time) with each page being made up of <b>tiles</b> that will always exactly fill the window. \n\n* Each tile can display a <b>patch</b>. There's only one kind of patch in mfp, and it corresponds to a single <tt>.mfp</tt> file.\n\n* Each patch can have <b>layers</b>. Only one layer can be displayed at a time in a patch."
        },
        "132": {
            "connections": [
                []
            ],
            "do_onload": false,
            "gui_params": {
                "clickchange": false,
                "code": null,
                "connection_info": [],
                "debug": false,
                "default": "",
                "display_type": "text",
                "export_offset_x": 0,
                "export_offset_y": 0,
                "height": 198.0,
                "is_export": false,
                "layername": "1.1 App layout",
                "max_width": 600,
                "min_height": 12,
                "min_width": 12,
                "no_export": false,
                "obj_args": null,
                "obj_name": "text_066",
                "obj_state": 3,
                "obj_type": "text",
                "panel_enable": false,
                "panel_x": 65.0,
                "panel_y": 393.0,
                "panel_z": 0,
                "patch_x": 65.0,
                "patch_y": 393.0,
                "patch_z": 0,
                "position_x": 65.0,
                "position_y": 393.0,
                "position_z": 0,
                "properties": {},
                "save_in_preset": false,
                "scope": "__patch__",
                "style": {},
                "update_required": true,
                "value": "<b>Log and Console</b>\n\nThe Log tab shows program output, status, and error messages. \n\nThe Console tab contains an interactive Python REPL that can be used for debugging and live-coding.\n\nToggle display of the Log and Console with the ~ (tilde) key or in the Window menu.\n\n<b>Status and Command Line</b>\n\nBelow the Log panel is a status line that shows status and notifications, and when input is needed from you the prompt will appear there. The default display shows tooltips from hovered items, or a summary of the current input mode if nothing is hovered.",
                "width": 599.0
            },
            "initargs": null,
            "midi_filters": null,
            "midi_mode": null,
            "name": "text_066",
            "osc_methods": [],
            "presets": {},
            "properties": {},
            "type": "text",
            "value": "<b>Log and Console</b>\n\nThe Log tab shows program output, status, and error messages. \n\nThe Console tab contains an interactive Python REPL that can be used for debugging and live-coding.\n\nToggle display of the Log and Console with the ~ (tilde) key or in the Window menu.\n\n<b>Status and Command Line</b>\n\nBelow the Log panel is a status line that shows status and notifications, and when input is needed from you the prompt will appear there. The default display shows tooltips from hovered items, or a summary of the current input mode if nothing is hovered."
        },
