        "28": {
            "connections": [
                []
            ],
            "do_onload": false,
            "gui_params": {
                "clickchange": false,
                "code": null,
                "connection_info": [],
                "debug": false,
                "default": "",
                "display_type": "text",
                "export_offset_x": 0,
                "export_offset_y": 0,
                "height": 531.0,
                "is_export": false,
                "layername": "6. Creating elements",
                "max_width": 600,
                "min_height": 527.1988269090652,
                "min_width": 430.59648072719574,
                "no_export": false,
                "obj_args": null,
                "obj_name": "text_044",
                "obj_state": 3,
                "obj_type": "text",
                "panel_enable": false,
                "panel_x": 43.0,
                "panel_y": 62.0,
                "panel_z": 0,
                "patch_x": 43.0,
                "patch_y": 62.0,
                "patch_z": 0,
                "position_x": 43.0,
                "position_y": 62.0,
                "position_z": 0,
                "properties": {},
                "save_in_preset": false,
                "scope": "__patch__",
                "style": {},
                "update_required": true,
                "value": "In the \"hello, world\" example you used <tt>m</tt> and <tt>p</tt> to create patch \nelements.  All the keys to create elements are part of Patch Edit mode, and they are: \n\n<b>Basics</b> \n\n| Cmd | Action |\n|-|-|\n| <tt>m</tt> | Create a message box |\n| <tt>p</tt> | Create a processor (user patch or builtin with no special UI) |\n| <tt>n</tt> | Create a number box |\n| <tt>t</tt> | Create a text comment |\n\n<b>Controls</b>\n\n| Cmd | Action |\n|-|-|\n|   <tt>u</tt>    | Create a toggle button |\n|   <tt>g</tt>    | Create a \"bang\" (momentary) button |\n|   <tt>s</tt>    | Create a slider|\n|   <tt>d</tt>   | Create a dial (round slider)|\n\n<b>Display</b>\n\n| Cmd | Action |\n|-|-|\n|   <tt>b</tt>   | Create a bar meter|\n|   <tt>i</tt>   | Create an indicator (on/off toggle display)|\n|   <tt>x</tt>   | X/Y plot (oscilloscope or scatter style) | ",
                "width": 438.0
            },
            "initargs": null,
            "midi_filters": null,
            "midi_mode": null,
            "name": "text_044",
            "osc_methods": [],
            "presets": {},
            "properties": {},
            "type": "text",
            "value": "In the \"hello, world\" example you used <tt>m</tt> and <tt>p</tt> to create patch \nelements.  All the keys to create elements are part of Patch Edit mode, and they are: \n\n<b>Basics</b> \n\n| Cmd | Action |\n|-|-|\n| <tt>m</tt> | Create a message box |\n| <tt>p</tt> | Create a processor (user patch or builtin with no special UI) |\n| <tt>n</tt> | Create a number box |\n| <tt>t</tt> | Create a text comment |\n\n<b>Controls</b>\n\n| Cmd | Action |\n|-|-|\n|   <tt>u</tt>    | Create a toggle button |\n|   <tt>g</tt>    | Create a \"bang\" (momentary) button |\n|   <tt>s</tt>    | Create a slider|\n|   <tt>d</tt>   | Create a dial (round slider)|\n\n<b>Display</b>\n\n| Cmd | Action |\n|-|-|\n|   <tt>b</tt>   | Create a bar meter|\n|   <tt>i</tt>   | Create an indicator (on/off toggle display)|\n|   <tt>x</tt>   | X/Y plot (oscilloscope or scatter style) | "
        },
