        "46": {
            "connections": [
                []
            ],
            "do_onload": false,
            "gui_params": {
                "clickchange": false,
                "code": null,
                "connection_info": [],
                "debug": false,
                "default": "",
                "display_type": "text",
                "export_offset_x": 0,
                "export_offset_y": 0,
                "height": 396.0,
                "is_export": false,
                "layername": "3. Edit and Operate",
                "max_width": 600,
                "min_height": 12,
                "min_width": 12,
                "no_export": false,
                "obj_args": null,
                "obj_name": "text_042",
                "obj_state": 3,
                "obj_type": "text",
                "panel_enable": false,
                "panel_x": 63.0,
                "panel_y": 87.0,
                "panel_z": 0,
                "patch_x": 63.0,
                "patch_y": 87.0,
                "patch_z": 0,
                "position_x": 63.0,
                "position_y": 87.0,
                "position_z": 0,
                "properties": {},
                "save_in_preset": false,
                "scope": "__patch__",
                "style": {},
                "update_required": true,
                "value": "On the left side of the status line, find the <b>mode indicator</b>.  It will say something like <b>Global > Edit</b> or <b>Global > Operate</b>. You may need to move the mouse pointer to an empty spot in the Canvas if a tooltip is showing instead. \n\nThis is the stack of \"input modes\" that MFP uses to interpret your key presses and mouse activity. \"Global\" mode is always active and includes actions that are always applicable, such as opening and saving files. The next mode after Global is the \"major mode\", which is always Edit or Operate. After that are \"minor modes\" which are enabled and disabled as needed. \n\n<b>Edit</b> mode allows you to create, move, and edit objects in the patch.  Your key presses and mouse activity are interpreted as editing operations.  MFP starts in <b>Edit</b> mode.  To re-enter <b>Edit</b> mode, type C-SPC (control-space). In Edit mode, the background of the currently-selected patch shows a grid pattern.\n\n<b>Operate</b> mode allows you to operate the patch.  Your inputs are interpreted as\ncontrol operations, and will not alter the patch.  You need to be in <b>Operate</b> mode to click buttons or messages, drag sliders, etc.  To enter <b>Operate</b> mode, hit ESC (escape). In Operate mode, the grid background of the selected patch is dimmed (lower contrast).\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Hit ESC to enter <b>Operate</b> mode.  Note the change in the Mode Indicator and the background\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Hit C-SPC to return to <b>Edit</b> mode",
                "width": 600.0
            },
            "initargs": null,
            "midi_filters": null,
            "midi_mode": null,
            "name": "text_042",
            "osc_methods": [],
            "presets": {},
            "properties": {},
            "type": "text",
            "value": "On the left side of the status line, find the <b>mode indicator</b>.  It will say something like <b>Global > Edit</b> or <b>Global > Operate</b>. You may need to move the mouse pointer to an empty spot in the Canvas if a tooltip is showing instead. \n\nThis is the stack of \"input modes\" that MFP uses to interpret your key presses and mouse activity. \"Global\" mode is always active and includes actions that are always applicable, such as opening and saving files. The next mode after Global is the \"major mode\", which is always Edit or Operate. After that are \"minor modes\" which are enabled and disabled as needed. \n\n<b>Edit</b> mode allows you to create, move, and edit objects in the patch.  Your key presses and mouse activity are interpreted as editing operations.  MFP starts in <b>Edit</b> mode.  To re-enter <b>Edit</b> mode, type C-SPC (control-space). In Edit mode, the background of the currently-selected patch shows a grid pattern.\n\n<b>Operate</b> mode allows you to operate the patch.  Your inputs are interpreted as\ncontrol operations, and will not alter the patch.  You need to be in <b>Operate</b> mode to click buttons or messages, drag sliders, etc.  To enter <b>Operate</b> mode, hit ESC (escape). In Operate mode, the grid background of the selected patch is dimmed (lower contrast).\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Hit ESC to enter <b>Operate</b> mode.  Note the change in the Mode Indicator and the background\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Hit C-SPC to return to <b>Edit</b> mode"
        },
