        "48": {
            "connections": [
                []
            ],
            "do_onload": false,
            "gui_params": {
                "clickchange": false,
                "code": null,
                "connection_info": [],
                "debug": false,
                "default": "",
                "display_type": "text",
                "export_offset_x": 0,
                "export_offset_y": 0,
                "height": 348.0,
                "is_export": false,
                "layername": "7. Zoom and scroll",
                "max_width": 600,
                "min_height": 12,
                "min_width": 12,
                "no_export": false,
                "obj_args": null,
                "obj_name": "text_043",
                "obj_state": 3,
                "obj_type": "text",
                "panel_enable": false,
                "panel_x": 66.0,
                "panel_y": 88.0,
                "panel_z": 0,
                "patch_x": 66.0,
                "patch_y": 88.0,
                "patch_z": 0,
                "position_x": 66.0,
                "position_y": 88.0,
                "position_z": 0,
                "properties": {},
                "save_in_preset": false,
                "scope": "__patch__",
                "style": {},
                "update_required": true,
                "value": "Zoom and scroll on the canvas are handled without scrollbars, using a \"drag\nto scroll and scroll to zoom\" model something like Google Maps.  \n\n<b>C-S-M1MOTION</b> is the keybinding to drag the canvas.  That is, hold \ndown the Control and Shift keys while clicking mouse button 1 (usually left) \nand dragging.  \n\n> [!TIP]\n> Use <b>C-S-M1MOTION</b> to drag the canvas around.\n\nThe scroll wheel zooms in and out.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Zoom!\n\n<b>C-0</b> (control-zero) resets the zoom to 1, and the position of the canvas to its starting point. \n\nThe <b>Window &gt; Magnification</b> submenu adjusts the overall app resolution, to compensate for display DPI differences or user preference. You can also adjust magnification with the <tt>--magnification</tt> command line argument.",
                "width": 595.0
            },
            "initargs": null,
            "midi_filters": null,
            "midi_mode": null,
            "name": "text_043",
            "osc_methods": [],
            "presets": {},
            "properties": {},
            "type": "text",
            "value": "Zoom and scroll on the canvas are handled without scrollbars, using a \"drag\nto scroll and scroll to zoom\" model something like Google Maps.  \n\n<b>C-S-M1MOTION</b> is the keybinding to drag the canvas.  That is, hold \ndown the Control and Shift keys while clicking mouse button 1 (usually left) \nand dragging.  \n\n> [!TIP]\n> Use <b>C-S-M1MOTION</b> to drag the canvas around.\n\nThe scroll wheel zooms in and out.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Zoom!\n\n<b>C-0</b> (control-zero) resets the zoom to 1, and the position of the canvas to its starting point. \n\nThe <b>Window &gt; Magnification</b> submenu adjusts the overall app resolution, to compensate for display DPI differences or user preference. You can also adjust magnification with the <tt>--magnification</tt> command line argument."
        },
