        "8": {
            "connections": [
                []
            ],
            "do_onload": false,
            "gui_params": {
                "clickchange": false,
                "code": null,
                "connection_info": [],
                "debug": false,
                "default": "",
                "display_type": "text",
                "export_offset_x": 0,
                "export_offset_y": 0,
                "height": 512.0,
                "is_export": false,
                "layername": "2. Creating a patch",
                "max_width": 600,
                "min_height": 508.8018248081207,
                "min_width": 393.3872263431549,
                "no_export": false,
                "obj_args": null,
                "obj_name": "text_015",
                "obj_state": 3,
                "obj_type": "text",
                "panel_enable": false,
                "panel_x": 65.0,
                "panel_y": 174.0,
                "panel_z": 0,
                "patch_x": 65.0,
                "patch_y": 174.0,
                "patch_z": 0,
                "position_x": 65.0,
                "position_y": 174.0,
                "position_z": 0,
                "properties": {},
                "save_in_preset": false,
                "scope": "__patch__",
                "style": {},
                "update_required": true,
                "value": "> [!TIP]\n> Type <b>C-f</b> now to create a new patch\n\nThis will only work if your pointer is over the \"canvas\". If it did, the main window should have been split vertically with a new empty tile on the right.\n\nClick on the Window menu to see that there's a new top-level item matching the name in the header of the new tile (either \"default\" or \"default_002\", most likely) This is \nyour working patch.\n\nThe <b>PGUP</b> and <b>PGDN</b> keys only cycle through layers in the current patch; to cycle \nthrough patches, <b>C-PGUP</b> and <b>C-PGDN</b>.  You can also select the patch in the <b>Window</b> menu, or just click in it.\n\n>[!TIP]\n> Select your working patch, then type <b>C-a !</b> (control-a followed by !) to move it to its own page in the UI. Then return to the tutorial via the Window menu or <b>C-a p</b> (go to previous page)\n\n",
                "width": 401.0
            },
            "initargs": null,
            "midi_filters": null,
            "midi_mode": null,
            "name": "text_015",
            "osc_methods": [],
            "presets": {},
            "properties": {},
            "type": "text",
            "value": "> [!TIP]\n> Type <b>C-f</b> now to create a new patch\n\nThis will only work if your pointer is over the \"canvas\". If it did, the main window should have been split vertically with a new empty tile on the right.\n\nClick on the Window menu to see that there's a new top-level item matching the name in the header of the new tile (either \"default\" or \"default_002\", most likely) This is \nyour working patch.\n\nThe <b>PGUP</b> and <b>PGDN</b> keys only cycle through layers in the current patch; to cycle \nthrough patches, <b>C-PGUP</b> and <b>C-PGDN</b>.  You can also select the patch in the <b>Window</b> menu, or just click in it.\n\n>[!TIP]\n> Select your working patch, then type <b>C-a !</b> (control-a followed by !) to move it to its own page in the UI. Then return to the tutorial via the Window menu or <b>C-a p</b> (go to previous page)\n\n"
        },
